# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores text as LF and lets Git hand each platform what it
# wants on checkout. That is already what a Windows clone with the default
# core.autocrlf=true does, but it is a property of whoever's git config happens
# to be in effect, and one contributor set differently is enough to commit CRLF
# into a file that then breaks for everyone else.
* text=auto

# The shell scripts are the case where it is not merely untidy. A CRLF in the
# first line makes the kernel look for an interpreter called "bash\r", and the
# error it produces -- "/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory" --
# names a file that plainly exists, which is a genuinely hard thing to read.
#
# eol=lf pins them in the *working tree* as well as in the repository, on every
# platform. That matters here beyond Linux: this project is developed on
# Windows, and running these scripts under WSL against the Windows working tree
# (/mnt/c/...) reads exactly the bytes Git checked out there.
*.sh    text eol=lf

# The PowerShell counterparts, for the same reason in the other direction:
# some older PowerShell hosts are unhappy with bare LF in a script.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png   binary
*.xlsx  binary
